+
+Build the new SQLite index in a temporary file. Stream the snapshot records into it, then verify record counts and the index's integrity check. Activate the new index only after all validation succeeds: a snapshot-date-stamped atomic rename makes it the active index and makes the lookup path read a complete index at every moment. An import failure deletes the temporary index, leaves the previous index active, and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. The active index stores serial numbers already normalized in the same way, so lookups are exact-match queries on a primary key; the 250 ms goal on a 90,000-record index is met by that single indexed read without any search-service dependency or network route. Return the snapshot date recorded in the index header even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index built per kiosk on snapshot arrival | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Predictable, fast lookups with no service to run and no runtime network use, matching the HLD tenet that lookups complete using files on the kiosk. Cost is per-kiosk index rebuild inside this component, which stays within the 150 MB snapshot budget. The HTTPS directory adds lab-network dependency and timeout handling for no lookup gain; the managed service adds recurring cost, credentials, and external connectivity. Certificates keep the same interface either way, so the choice stays inside this node. |
